name markdawn

source {
    url "git@github.com:necessary-nu/markdawn.git"
    ref main
}

children (
    {
        id markdawn-highlighter-boundary
        title "Establish the renderer-neutral syntax-highlighting boundary"
        priority 0
        implements {
            main (
                [spec:mw:req:highlighting.api]
                [spec:mw:req:highlighting.span-semantics]
                [spec:mw:req:highlighting.error-fallback]
                [spec:mw:req:highlighting.arborium-transition]
            )
        }
        log (
            {
                at 2026-07-29T12:50:59.623254Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-07-29T19:22:36.683666Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Scope widened by the operator to include migrating the grammar backend from arborium to ntreesitter (../ntreesitter, described as a saner port of arborium). Rationale: this node already owns highlighting.arborium-transition, and the renderer-neutral SyntaxHighlighter / SyntaxSpan / HighlightError seam it establishes is exactly the boundary a backend swap goes through — doing the swap as a separate node would mean touching every renderer twice. Footprint at the time of this decision: 26 first-party arborium call sites (src/highlight.rs, src/lib.rs, src/tracey/render.rs, crates/markdawn-android/{lib,grammar_store}.rs), 104 generated markdawn-swift-lang-* crates which are gitignored and produced by crates/markdawn-swift/generate-plugins.sh (so the template is the real edit, not 104 crates), plus the highlight / highlight-all-languages cargo features and the path dependency on ../arborium. ntreesitter is structured differently — nine crates including -plugin, -provider, -catalog, -runtime and -wire — so the plugin registration protocol spec'd as ffi.plugin.grammar-registration (mw_lang_&lt;name&gt;_init(RegisterFn)) is expected to change shape rather than just names. A read-only API survey was commissioned before any code change; plan the migration from its findings. Note this node is claimed by a different session, so the scope change was recorded here rather than acted on unilaterally."
            }

            {
                at 2026-07-29T19:27:32.101127Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Read-only API survey of ntreesitter vs arborium, findings for planning the backend swap.\n\nBLOCKER — grammar coverage. arborium ships 104 grammars; ntreesitter-catalog ships 11 (c, cpp, html, javascript, json, lean, markdown, rust, silo, tsx, typescript). 93 missing, including bash, python, go, java, ruby, php, swift, kotlin, css, yaml, toml, sql. imports/inventory.json shows a deliberate staged import from arborium at revision 2d7ac7f1 with blake3-pinned provenance, so this is a backlog rather than an oversight — but the import cadence is not determinable from the repos and no schedule is credible until it is answered.\n\nPLUGIN PROTOCOL IS A REDESIGN, NOT A RENAME. Per-language `mw_lang_<name>_init(RegisterFn)` becomes one universal `ntreesitter_provider_v1()` returning a repr(C) descriptor; the callback handoff inverts (host pulls a descriptor rather than the plugin calling mw_register_grammar, which has no counterpart). The single-symbol model breaks the current loader design — you cannot dlsym the same symbol from 104 cdylibs without per-handle resolution, so the Swift and JNI loaders need reworking. Descriptor surface is much wider than the current template emits: aliases, extensions, exact filenames, injection targets, parser ABI and provenance hashes, none of which exist in arborium's per-language crates. ntreesitter defines the ABI but ships no dynamic loader — that stays markdawn's job.\n\nNO HTML EMITTER. arborium::advanced::spans_to_html has no ntreesitter equivalent; markdawn must own a span-to-HTML serializer (src/highlight.rs:118-127).\n\nSPAN SEMANTICS — one real divergence. Byte offsets, half-open ranges, raw capture names and nesting all survive. But `priority` is NOT arborium's `pattern_index`: it is a monotonic emission-order counter assigned after the upstream engine resolves query precedence, not highlights.scm line order. Losing captures are never surfaced, so re-deciding equal-range captures by query order is gone rather than renamed. Partially-overlapping non-nested spans cannot occur (strict stack), making the word \"overlapping\" in highlighting.span-semantics inaccurate.\n\nOTHER DESIGN DECISIONS NEEDED. Registry construction is fallible and rejects ambiguous aliases at build time, where arborium tolerated collisions — with 104 merged alias sets, collisions are likely and unpredictable until descriptors exist. DynamicRegistry hands out immutable generation-tracked snapshots, so VdomPipeline pinning a store at construction will silently not see late-registered languages. HighlightOutput carries a new `diagnostics` vec for non-fatal partial-injection failures that the current boundary cannot express. ntreesitter vendors tree-sitter 0.26.8 with links = \"tree-sitter\" and pins tree-sitter-language =0.1.7, while markdawn's swift lang crates depend on 0.1 — check for a links collision early.\n\nSPEC RULES NEEDING REWRITE: highlighting.span-semantics (priority and overlapping wording), uikit.syntax.span-priority (names pattern_index explicitly), highlighting.arborium-transition (retire or generalise to provider-transition), highlighting.api (needs a diagnostics clause). uikit.syntax.capture-fallback and span-bounds are unaffected.\n\nBUILD: ntreesitter generates parsers at build time from grammar.json + scanner.c with blake3 verification rather than shipping ~100 MB of pre-generated static data, so build time rises and artifact size falls, per-crate. Feature story maps cleanly (catalog has one feature per language, default all). wasm is first-class and spec'd, unlike arborium."
            }
        )
    }

    {
